==Middle French==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|frm|fro|tot}}.

===Adjective===
{{frm-adj|toute|tous|toutes}}

# [[all]]; all of
#: {{ux|frm|'''toute''' la nuit|t=all (of the) night}}

===Adverb===
{{head|frm|adverb|feminine singular|toute|masculine plural|tous|feminine plural|toutes}}

# [[all]] (intensifier)
#* {{quote-book|frm|year=1488|author=Jean Dupré|title=Lancelot du Lac|passage=Et moult y avoit de gens '''tout''' autour pour regarder la iustice de la damoiselle|page=[http://gallica.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/bpt6k111060r/f45.item.zoom 45]|translation=And there were many people all around to watch the justice afforded to the lady}}
# [[completely]]; [[totally]]; [[entirely]]

====Usage notes====
* Like Modern French {{m|fr|tout}}, when used as an intensifier it may inflect according to the gender and the number of what it is describing:
*: {{ux|frm|Elle est '''toute''' morte|t=she is completely dead|inline=1}}
* The uninflected form ''tout'' is always used for describing terms that don't inflect with gender, such as verbs, adverbs and prepositions:
*: {{ux|frm|y avoit de gens '''tout''' autour|t=there were people all around|inline=1}} (''tout'' qualifies the preposition {{m|frm|autour}})

